sharedContract:
{"academicContract":"Teach Grade 3 multiplication through fresh material:
- interpret multiplicative situations as number of equal groups and amount in each group;
- construct or read equal-group and array representations;
- connect the representation to a multiplication equation and product;
- increase mathematical independence and challenge over the experience.

The experience must remain mathematically correct, usable with touch or mouse in a 1365×768 iframe, and complete from opening through payoff. Everything else is creatively mutable.","experiment":"reina-persona-staging-comparison-v1","factualChildEvidence":[{"evidenceId":"reina:age:2026-07","fact":"The child is currently nine years old."},{"evidenceId":"reina:conversation:wrestling","fact":"She independently chose wrestling competition and a state-championship loss as a conversation topic."},{"evidenceId":"reina:riddles:harder","fact":"She asked to continue difficult riddles across sessions and later explicitly requested harder riddles."},{"evidenceId":"reina:riddles:logic","fact":"She immediately noticed repeated riddles and questioned faulty riddle logic."},{"evidenceId":"reina:riddles:scaffold","fact":"When stuck on a multi-step logic problem, she requested hints and continued after scaffolding."},{"evidenceId":"reina:activity-feedback:small-sample","fact":"Small-sample human and child feedback was positive for a randomized reveal spelling activity and a spoken-pronunciation activity."}],"requirements":"Return one complete standalone HTML document and nothing else. Implement the frozen curriculum and design in a 1365×768 iframe. It must be immediately understandable to a nine-year-old, work with touch or mouse, visibly react to correct and incorrect actions, progress through a complete experience, and deliver the promised payoff. External HTTPS libraries and assets are allowed. Do not access Sunny APIs, cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, child data, or currency. Do not use speech synthesis or raw oscillator audio.","stage":"experience-engineer","upstream":{"curriculum":{"charterConcern":"Two open concerns, both flagged rather than resolved here. First, the responsibility-transfer point is fixed at item-04 by charter progression, but asm-05 is only 0.6 confidence and no supplied evidence addresses construction versus interpretation in mathematics at all. If the construct-vs-read accuracy delta at item-04 proves large, the transfer likely needs an intermediate rung — a partially pre-built representation the learner completes — which this packet does not currently contain and which would require a sixth instructional item. Second, and more structurally: the charter directs a monotonically rising ramp with an optional escalation (asm-02, 0.65 confidence) on the strength of a learner requesting harder riddles. That is verbal-puzzle evidence, and the charter itself names the risk that in mathematics difficulty can read as failure rather than invitation. I have written item-06 as strictly elective with a preserved-progress exit, but I want to note that the escalation offer's framing is a downstream creative decision I do not control, and a competitively framed offer could plausibly discourage rather than motivate. Relatedly, the wrestling-loss evidence underwriting asm-06 (0.5 confidence) establishes only comfort discussing a competitive loss, not any in-task failure response; the retry design should be treated as an untested hypothesis, not a settled accommodation. I have also kept item-05's referent note informal per the out-of-scope rule against formal property names, but describing 10 x 3 as counting shelf-groups of books requires careful downstream wording to stay unambiguous — this is exactly the kind of phrasing asm-04 warns carries trust cost if it lands loosely.","items":[{"academicTarget":"Interpret a multiplicative situation as a number of equal groups and an amount in each group, naming both quantities explicitly with correct referents.","correctAnswer":{"amountInEachGroup":3,"groupReferent":"trays","memberReferent":"apple slices per tray","numberOfGroups":4},"exposure":{"factorRange":"2-5","factors":[4,3],"learnerConstructs":"nothing; interpretation only","novelty":"first appearance of group/amount-per-group language","product":12,"representationType":"equal groups (given, complete)","scaffoldDensity":"maximum: both quantities offered as bounded choices, no product demanded"},"itemId":"item-01-open-orientation","options":{"amountPerGroupChoices":[2,3,4,5],"groupCountChoices":[2,3,4,5]},"prompt":"Four lunch trays are set out. Each tray holds 3 apple slices. Before finding any total, answer the two questions that govern every item here: How many equal groups are there? How many are in each group?","responseContract":{"hintPolicy":"none needed; no scoring, no failure state in orientation","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: learner is invited to re-count the trays or the slices on one tray in the shown situation; progress is preserved and the item is re-answered, not skipped.","inputMode":"single tap or click on one choice per question; both questions visible simultaneously without scrolling","learnerSupplies":["numberOfGroups","amountInEachGroup"],"productRequested":false,"type":"two-part-selection","validation":"Both parts must be committed before any feedback. Feedback names the referent back to the learner ("4 trays, 3 slices on each tray") and never states the product at this stage."},"segment":"Opening orientation","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-01-grade-band-fit","asm-07-interaction-modality"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored situation; not drawn from or adapted from any worksheet item.","progressionStep":"Opening orientation: establish the two governing questions with factors at or below 5, no scoring, no failure state"}},{"academicTarget":"Determine the product from a given equal-group representation and its matching equation using counting, skip counting, or repeated addition, and verify it against the representation.","correctAnswer":{"equation":"5 x 4 = 20","product":20,"verification":"Directly countable: 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 + 4 = 20 tulips shown."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"2-5","factors":[5,4],"learnerConstructs":"the product only","novelty":"first product production; first skip-counting demand","product":20,"representationType":"equal groups (given, complete) with matching equation shown","scaffoldDensity":"high: both factors and equation given"},"itemId":"item-02-seg1-supply-product","options":{"productChoices":[9,16,20,25]},"prompt":"Five flower pots are shown. Each pot has exactly 4 tulips. The equation 5 x 4 = ? is shown beneath the pots, where 5 stands for the number of pots and 4 stands for the tulips in each pot. Count or skip count the tulips and supply the product.","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":true,"hintPolicy":"on request only, stepwise: (1) point at one pot and count its tulips; (2) count how many pots; (3) add that amount once for each pot. Hint never states 20.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: the representation stays on screen and the learner is asked to skip count aloud or in sequence by 4s across the pots, then re-answer. Progress preserved.","inputMode":"one tap or click on a product choice; choice targets sized for low-precision touch","learnerSupplies":["product"],"type":"single-selection-product","validation":"Feedback affirms only the countable product. A correct product is never rejected; an incorrect product is never affirmed."},"segment":"Segment 1 — read and count","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-01-grade-band-fit","asm-03-on-request-scaffolding","asm-06-persistence-after-setback"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; distractors chosen as 4+5 (added instead of multiplied), 4x4 (miscounted groups), 5x5 (miscounted amount) so each error is diagnostic. Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Segment 1 — representation and matching equation given, learner supplies only the product; factors 2-5"}},{"academicTarget":"Read a rectangular array as rows x amount in each row, identify what each factor refers to, and translate the representation into a multiplication equation and product.","correctAnswer":{"amountInEachRow":7,"equation":"6 x 7 = 42","numberOfRows":6,"product":42,"referents":{"6":"rows in the array","7":"stickers in each row"},"verification":"Directly countable: 7 stickers per row across 6 rows totals 42 stickers shown."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"2-7","factors":[6,7],"learnerConstructs":"both factors with referents, and the product","novelty":"first array; first rows-and-columns language; first unaided factor naming","product":42,"representationType":"rectangular array (given, complete)","scaffoldDensity":"medium: representation given, no equation given"},"itemId":"item-03-seg2-name-factors-array","options":{"perRowChoices":[5,6,7,8],"productChoices":[13,36,42,49],"rowsChoices":[5,6,7,8]},"prompt":"A complete rectangular array of stickers is shown with 6 rows and 7 stickers in every row. No equation is shown. Identify both factors with what each one refers to, then give the product.","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":false,"hintPolicy":"on request only, stepwise: (1) how many rows do you see; (2) count the stickers in just one row — every row has the same number; (3) write it as rows x amount in each row. Hint never states 42.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: one row is highlighted for re-counting, then the row count is highlighted; the array remains complete and unaltered. Progress preserved.","inputMode":"three tap or click selections, each on separately sized targets, all reachable without scrolling in a 1365x768 frame","learnerSupplies":["numberOfRows","amountInEachRow","product"],"type":"three-part-selection","validation":"Both factors must be committed with correct referents before the product is accepted. The equation is not revealed until the learner has committed to both factors."},"segment":"Segment 2 — name the factors","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-01-grade-band-fit","asm-05-independence-transfer","asm-04-logical-integrity-sensitivity"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; array is a complete rectangle with uniform rows and columns. Distractors: 6+7 (addition), 6x6 and 7x7 (single-factor confusion). Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Segment 2 — representation given, learner identifies both factors with referents and produces the product; introduces rows-and-columns language; factors 2-7"}},{"academicTarget":"Construct a faithful equal-group or array representation from a verbal multiplicative situation, then translate it into a multiplication equation with correct factor referents and a verified product.","correctAnswer":{"builtRepresentation":"a complete 8-by-6 rectangular array (8 rows, 6 in each row), or an equivalent equal-groups arrangement of 8 groups of 6","equation":"8 x 6 = 48","product":48,"referents":{"6":"carrots in each row","8":"rows of carrots"},"verification":"Directly countable: the learner's own built representation contains 48 carrot markers."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"3-9","factors":[8,6],"learnerConstructs":"the representation, both factors, and the product","novelty":"first construction with no model shown; first time no representation is supplied","product":48,"representationType":"learner-constructed (array or equal groups; both valid)","scaffoldDensity":"low: no representation, no equation, structural integrity guard only"},"itemId":"item-04-seg3-construct-from-words","options":{"buildSurface":"tap-to-place grid; learner sets the number of rows and the number placed in each row","equationAssembly":{"leftFactorTiles":[4,6,8,9],"rightFactorTiles":[4,6,8,9]},"productChoices":[14,36,48,54]},"prompt":"Words only, no picture: A gardener plants 8 rows of carrots with 6 carrots in each row. Build a faithful representation of this situation on the empty grid surface, then form the equation and give the product.","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":false,"hintPolicy":"on request only, stepwise: (1) find the number of equal groups or rows in the sentence; (2) find how many are in each one; (3) place that many, once for each row, then read your build as an equation. Hint never states 48.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: the learner is asked which number in the words tells how many rows and which tells how many in each row, then may rebuild. The built work is preserved, not erased, and progress is kept.","inputMode":"single taps or clicks to place rows and members; no drag precision required; equation assembled by tapping factor tiles","learnerSupplies":["representation","leftFactor","rightFactor","product"],"type":"construct-then-equation-then-product","validation":"The system preserves structural integrity — an unequal group or a ragged row cannot be silently committed — but it never auto-arranges the correct array and never auto-fills a factor or the product. The learner decides the structure. Both an 8-rows-of-6 array and an 8-groups-of-6 equal-groups build are accepted as faithful."},"segment":"Segment 3 — construct","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-05-independence-transfer","asm-03-on-request-scaffolding","asm-07-interaction-modality"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; the responsibility-transfer point where the model is no longer supplied. Distractors: 8+6 (addition), 6x6 and 9x6 (factor misread). Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Segment 3 — situation in words only, learner constructs the representation then the equation and product; factors 3-9, products to 90"}},{"academicTarget":"Recognize that the same representation and product can be written with factors in either order, that swapping factors changes what each factor refers to but not the product, and reject equations that do not match the representation.","correctAnswer":{"matching":["3 x 10 = 30","10 x 3 = 30"],"notMatching":["3 + 10 = 13","10 x 10 = 100"],"referentNote":"In 3 x 10 the 3 counts the shelves and the 10 counts the books on each shelf. In 10 x 3 the numbers are read in the other order, so 10 counts books in each shelf-group and 3 counts the shelves; the total is the same 30 books either way. The order changes what each factor is read as standing for, not the product.","verification":"Directly countable: 10 + 10 + 10 = 30 books shown on the three shelves."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"2-10","factors":[3,10],"learnerConstructs":"the judgment set and the referent explanation","novelty":"first multi-select judgment; first explicit treatment of factor order and referent","product":30,"representationType":"equal groups (given) judged against multiple candidate equations","scaffoldDensity":"low: representation given, but truth value of each equation is entirely learner-decided"},"itemId":"item-05-seg4-judge-matching-equations","options":{"equationChoices":["3 x 10 = 30","10 x 3 = 30","3 + 10 = 13","10 x 10 = 100"]},"prompt":"One representation is shown: 3 shelves with 10 books standing on each shelf. Four equations are listed. Select every equation that correctly matches this representation, and leave unselected any that does not. For each one you select, be ready to say what each factor refers to.","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":true,"hintPolicy":"on request only, stepwise: (1) count the total books directly; (2) ask whether each equation's numbers describe groups and amount per group here; (3) check whether the equation's total equals your count. Hint never lists the answer set.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: the shelves stay on screen and the learner re-checks one equation at a time against the countable books, then re-commits. Progress preserved.","inputMode":"tap or click to toggle each equation on or off, then one tap to commit; toggles are large, keyboard-free, and hover-free","learnerSupplies":["set of matching equations"],"type":"multi-select-judgment","validation":"Partial selections are not auto-completed. Feedback addresses each equation the learner judged, affirming both valid orderings and never affirming 3 + 10 = 13 or 10 x 10 = 100 as a match. No formal property name is required or used."},"segment":"Segment 4 — flexible structure","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-04-logical-integrity-sensitivity","asm-05-independence-transfer"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; deliberately includes two valid orderings so no single structure is presented as the only correct model. Distractors are an addition confusion and a squared-factor confusion. Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Segment 4 — same product by different valid structures; judge which equations match a representation, surfacing factor-order referent without formal property naming; factors 2-10"}},{"academicTarget":"Sustain multiplicative reasoning across a multi-step array situation with factors to 10, re-identifying the number of equal rows and the amount in each row when the situation changes, and verifying each product against the representation.","correctAnswer":{"fullSection":{"equation":"9 x 9 = 81","product":81,"referents":{"first 9":"rows in the section","second 9":"seats in each row"}},"uncovered":{"equation":"7 x 9 = 63","product":63,"referents":{"7":"uncovered rows","9":"seats in each row"}},"verification":"Directly countable: the full array shows 81 seats; with 2 of the 9 rows covered, 7 rows of 9 remain, totalling 63 countable pennants."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"7-10","factors":[9,9,7,9],"learnerConstructs":"both products, both equations, and all referents","novelty":"first multi-step item; largest factors in the experience; learner-elected entry","products":[81,63],"representationType":"rectangular array (given, complete) with a partial-view second stage","scaffoldDensity":"minimal: nothing pushed, hints only on request"},"itemId":"item-06-seg5-elected-challenge","options":{"fullSectionProductChoices":[18,72,81,90],"uncoveredProductChoices":[49,56,63,72]},"prompt":"Optional harder tier, chosen by you: A stadium section has 9 rows of seats with 9 seats in each row. Every seat holds exactly 1 pennant. Two rows are then covered by a banner and their seats are not counted. First give the product for the full 9-row section, then give how many pennants are still countable in the uncovered rows. Give both factors and their referents for each answer.","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":false,"hintPolicy":"on request only, stepwise, and only in this tier: (1) how many rows are there in all; (2) how many rows are left once 2 are covered; (3) each remaining row still holds the same number of seats. Hint never states 81 or 63.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry at the stage that was missed only; the other stage's committed correct work is preserved. The learner may return to the standard tier at any time without losing completed progress.","inputMode":"tap or click selections at each stage; the array remains fully visible in the 1365x768 frame without scrolling","learnerSupplies":["fullSectionProduct","uncoveredProduct","factor referents for both stages"],"type":"two-stage-multi-step","validation":"Stage two is not unlocked by the system supplying stage one's answer; the learner must commit stage one first. Hints appear only if requested — nothing is pushed in this tier."},"segment":"Segment 5 — learner-elected challenge (optional)","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-02-rising-challenge-ramp","asm-03-on-request-scaffolding","asm-06-persistence-after-setback"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; multi-step but strictly multiplicative — the second stage is a new equal-group count (7 rows of 9), not a division or subtraction-of-products requirement. Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Segment 5 — optional harder tier with multi-step multiplicative situations or larger factors up to 10 x 10, hints on request only, no auto-scaffolding"}},{"academicTarget":"Restate the full multiplicative chain — situation to groups and amount per group, to representation, to equation, to verified product — and state what each factor referred to in the learner's own completed work.","correctAnswer":{"books":"3 counted the shelves, 10 counted the books on each shelf","carrots":"8 counted the rows, 6 counted the carrots in each row","chainRestated":"Situation, then how many equal groups and how many in each group, then a faithful representation, then the equation, then the product — checked by counting the representation."},"exposure":{"factorRange":"3-10 (revisited, not new)","factors":[8,6,3,10],"learnerConstructs":"the stated meaning of each factor","novelty":"no new mathematics; meaning retrieval only","products":[48,30],"representationType":"the learner's own prior constructions and judgments, re-shown","scaffoldDensity":"none pushed; the learner supplies meaning unaided"},"itemId":"item-07-payoff-consolidation","options":{"bookFactorMeanings":["3 counted the shelves, 10 counted the books on each shelf","3 counted the books on each shelf, 10 counted the shelves","3 and 10 were both totals"],"carrotFactorMeanings":["8 counted the rows, 6 counted the carrots in each row","8 counted the carrots in each row, 6 counted the rows","8 and 6 were both totals"]},"prompt":"Your own work, replayed: for the carrot rows you built yourself and the shelves of books you judged, state what each factor stood for. In your 8 x 6 = 48 build, what did the 8 count and what did the 6 count? In 3 x 10 = 30, what did the 3 count and what did the 10 count?","responseContract":{"factorsGiven":true,"hintPolicy":"on request only: re-highlight one row or one group in the learner's own saved representation.","incorrectPath":"Reasoning retry: the learner's own representation is re-shown and the learner re-reads which number told how many groups and which told how many in each group. Unlimited re-answers; the experience always reaches its close.","inputMode":"one tap or click per statement; the learner's own earlier build and judgment are shown alongside, unaltered","learnerSupplies":["factor referent for each of two of the learner's own completed items"],"type":"anchored-meaning-check","validation":"This is a meaning check, not a scored gate; it cannot end the experience in a failure state. Feedback restates the full chain anchored to the learner's own completed work rather than a generic example, and never introduces a product that was not countable in that work."},"segment":"Payoff consolidation","sourceLineage":{"assumptionIds":["asm-08-payoff-as-consolidation","asm-04-logical-integrity-sensitivity"],"charterId":"charter:g3-multiplication-equal-groups-arrays:v1","derivation":"Freshly authored; references only items 04 and 05 of this packet so no new mathematics is introduced at the close and no item is repeated. Not worksheet-derived.","progressionStep":"Payoff consolidation: learner-visible restatement of the full chain anchored to the learner's own completed work"}}],"packetId":"packet:g3-mult-equal-groups-arrays:curriculum:v1","supportProgression":["Opening orientation (item-01): maximum support. Both governing quantities are offered as bounded choices, no product is demanded, no scoring and no failure state exists. Support here is structural rather than requested — it establishes vocabulary, not performance.","Segment 1 (item-02): high support. Representation and matching equation are both supplied; the learner owns only the product. Stepwise hints become available but are surfaced as an offer the learner may take, never auto-pushed. Hint tier 1 identifies one group, tier 2 counts the groups, tier 3 prompts repeated addition; no tier ever states the product.","Segment 2 (item-03): medium support. The equation is withdrawn — the learner supplies both factors with referents before any equation is revealed. Array language is introduced with the representation still fully given, so exactly one new demand is added at a time. Hints remain on request only and stepwise.","Segment 3 (item-04): low support and the responsibility-transfer point. The representation itself is withdrawn; the learner builds it. The system holds structural integrity (equal groups stay equal, rows stay uniform) but never auto-arranges, never auto-fills a factor, and never reveals the equation before the learner commits. Both an array and an equal-groups build are accepted, so no single structure is presented as the only valid model.","Segment 4 (item-05): low support with judgment shifted entirely to the learner. The representation returns as a reference surface, but the correctness of each candidate equation is the learner's decision, including two valid factor orderings. Hints direct attention to counting and checking, never to the answer set.","Segment 5 (item-06): minimal support, entered only by learner election. Multi-step reasoning with the largest factors; nothing is pushed at any point. Hints exist but must be requested, and stage two cannot be unlocked by the system answering stage one. The learner may exit back to the standard tier without losing progress.","Payoff (item-07): support returns only as retrieval assistance anchored to the learner's own saved work. No new mathematics, unlimited re-answers, and no failure exit — the experience always reaches its close.","Across all segments: every incorrect response routes to a reasoning retry that preserves progress rather than terminating the item. Retries re-expose the countable representation and ask the learner to re-decide, never supplying the decision. Scaffold density decreases monotonically from item-01 through item-06 while learner-constructed content increases; no item repeats within the packet."],"upstreamHash":"340b301444e0d2dc57ee8061b365ca552c03268105fa47d7b950e72b28267c1a"},"design":{"artifactId":"experience:g3-mult-equal-groups-arrays:signal-tower:v1","audienceRationale":"The learner is nine, chose a competitive wrestling loss as a conversation topic (comfort with stakes, not proof of in-task resilience), explicitly requested harder riddles across sessions, noticed repeated riddles instantly, and challenged faulty riddle logic. This profile argues for: (1) a world with real internal logic that never cheats — every product must be visibly countable, because she audits logic; (2) fresh material with zero repeated situations, because she detects repetition and it costs trust; (3) escalation framed as invitation and curiosity ('there's a bigger circuit if you want it') rather than competition or ranking, because the wrestling-loss evidence does not establish that failure-framing motivates her in math and the charter explicitly flags this risk; (4) hints that are dignified and stepwise on request, matching her demonstrated pattern of asking for scaffolding and then continuing — hints are a tool she reaches for, never a rescue pushed at her. The theme (restoring power to a valley by wiring equal-group energy circuits) is deliberately non-babyish: it treats her as a competent engineer, consistent with a child who asks for harder problems.","consequences":"Correct: current flows, the district illuminates lamp-by-lamp in the product's exact count, the tower gains one steady beacon ring, and the valley map's silhouette brightens — cumulative, visible, and permanent within the session. Incorrect: the current flickers at the switch and returns without any breakage sound, alarm, red flash, or lost progress; the world responds as a patient circuit tester, not a judge. Each incorrect commit triggers exactly the packet's incorrectPath for that item: the representation stays (or, in item-04, the learner's build is preserved untouched), one relevant element is highlighted for re-counting or re-checking, and the learner re-decides. Feedback language names referents ('4 trays, 3 slices on each tray'), never states withheld products, never affirms a wrong answer, never rejects a right one, and never introduces shame vocabulary — the circuit 'isn't carrying yet,' it is never 'wrong, try again.'","coreInteraction":"Tap-to-choose and tap-to-place, never drag-precision, per the packet's input contracts. Each item is one district's circuit panel: the countable representation (trays, pots, sticker array, build grid, shelves, stadium) occupies the left ~60% of the 1365×768 frame; the response panel (choices, factor tiles, equation slots, or the multi-select toggles) occupies the right ~40%, all reachable without scrolling. Item-04's construction uses a tap-to-place grid: tap a row-start cell to open a row, tap to set how many carrots fill it; the system enforces uniform rows (structural integrity guard) but never auto-arranges or auto-fills. Committing any answer routes power: correct commits close the switch and send visible current through the wire into the district; the district's lights come on room by room in exactly the product's count (e.g., 20 tulip-lamps flare in the greenhouse for item-02), so the product is re-verified by the world's own reaction. Incorrect commits never punish — the current travels the wire, reaches the district, flickers, and returns, and the scene enters the packet-specified reasoning retry (re-count a highlighted row, re-check one equation) with all progress preserved.","decisionRecords":[{"alternativesConsidered":["Bakery/party themes (rejected: too young-skewing and over-used for equal groups)","Wrestling-tournament theme (rejected: the wrestling evidence is conversational context, not a durable preference conclusion, and competitive framing conflicts with the charter's difficulty-as-failure warning)"],"decision":"Frame the experience as restoring power to a dark valley, with multiplication as the literal electrical law of the world.","decisionId":"dr-01-theme-selection","evidenceIds":["reina:riddles:harder","reina:age:2026-07"],"rationale":"The academic contract demands mathematics visibly control the world. A circuit metaphor makes groups × amount-per-group causally load-bearing: factors wire the circuit, the product is the countable wattage. It is also age-appropriately competent for a nine-year-old who requests harder challenges, avoiding babyish framing."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Champion/boss-level framing (rejected per charter concern)","Hiding item-06 until standard completion (rejected: removes the legible whole-scope promise and makes election feel like an afterthought)"],"decision":"Frame item-06 as a curiosity invitation ('the valley's biggest circuit — yours if you want it') with zero competitive, ranked, or test language, a standing reserve-circuit glow if declined, and a no-cost exit.","decisionId":"dr-02-escalation-framing","evidenceIds":["reina:riddles:harder","reina:conversation:wrestling"],"rationale":"The charter explicitly flags that the escalation offer's framing is a downstream creative decision and that competitive framing could discourage. asm-02 rests on verbal-puzzle evidence at 0.65 confidence. Curiosity framing preserves the invitation while eliminating the identified discouragement pathway; the standard path still yields a fully restored valley so declining carries no visible penalty."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Encouraging cheerleader feedback (rejected: risks reading as condescending to a challenge-seeking nine-year-old)","Explicit error explanations (rejected: packet incorrectPaths require the learner to re-decide; the system never supplies the decision)"],"decision":"Incorrect commits produce a neutral flicker-and-return with a diagnostic highlight — no red, no shake, no sad audio, no consoling language.","decisionId":"dr-03-failure-feedback-tone","evidenceIds":["reina:conversation:wrestling","reina:riddles:scaffold"],"rationale":"asm-06 is only 0.5 confidence and the retry design is an untested hypothesis; the safest tone is one that carries no failure semantics at all. The circuit 'isn't carrying yet' frames a miss as diagnostic information, which also suits a learner who audits logic and would resent both punishment and condescension."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Text-only referent note (rejected: highest loose-phrasing risk)","Character-narrated analogy (rejected: introduces unverifiable claims a logic-auditing child could contest)"],"decision":"Demonstrate factor-order equivalence by re-tracing the identical 30 book-lights in two reading orders rather than by verbal explanation.","decisionId":"dr-04-item05-referent-demonstration","evidenceIds":["reina:riddles:logic"],"rationale":"The charter warns that the shelf-groups phrasing carries trust cost if it lands loosely (asm-04), and the learner questions faulty logic. A countable visual demonstration — same 30 lights, two traversal orders, total unchanged — makes the referent swap verifiable by eye without formal property names, staying inside the packet's referentNote exactly."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Auto-surfacing hints after N misses (rejected: violates on-request hintPolicy and the packet's never-push rule in item-06)","No visible hint affordance until first miss (rejected: hides a tool she is known to use proactively)"],"decision":"Present hints as a small 'circuit notes' tool toggle — visually an engineer's instrument, available as an offer in items 02–05 and 07, strictly on-request in item-06, never auto-pushed anywhere.","decisionId":"dr-05-hint-affordance-design","evidenceIds":["reina:riddles:scaffold"],"rationale":"The learner's demonstrated pattern is requesting hints when stuck and continuing after scaffolding; the design must make requesting dignified. Framing hints as an engineer's tool rather than a help lifeline preserves her sense of competence while honoring every item's hintPolicy verbatim, including the rule that no tier states a withheld product."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Single reusable classroom scene (rejected: reads as repetition to a repetition-sensitive learner)","Procedurally varied numbers on replay (rejected: forbidden — mathematics is immutable)"],"decision":"Give each item a distinct district, distinct countable objects, distinct micro-mechanics within one constant interaction grammar, and rotate only mutable-layer elements on replay.","decisionId":"dr-06-repetition-and-freshness","evidenceIds":["reina:riddles:harder","reina:riddles:logic"],"rationale":"The learner immediately notices repeated content and it costs trust. All seven packet items are already fresh and non-repeating; the experience layer must match that standard — no reused scenes, no reskinned duplicates — while questions, answers, scope, and difficulty stay frozen per the requirements."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Auto-playing narration (rejected: interrupts deliberation and fails silently when muted)","No audio (rejected: forfeits the skip-counting rhythm support at item-02, the identified engagement risk window)"],"decision":"Provide optional tap-to-speak narration for all prompt and feedback text, with counting ticks that make group sizes audible, but keep the full experience functional with sound off.","decisionId":"dr-07-audio-narration","evidenceIds":["reina:activity-feedback:small-sample"],"rationale":"Small-sample feedback was positive for a spoken-pronunciation activity, suggesting spoken audio is welcome but not proven essential; making it optional-by-tap captures the upside with no dependency risk in a possibly muted iframe."},{"alternativesConsidered":["Smuggling a pre-built partial array into item-04's retry path (rejected: alters scaffold density and violates the immutable-mathematics constraint)","Silently ignoring the concerns (rejected: destroys the audit trail the staged pipeline depends on)"],"decision":"Flag rather than resolve both open charter concerns: no intermediate construction rung is added at item-04, and the item-04 accuracy delta plus item-06 election behavior are named as falsifying evidence for upstream review.","decisionId":"dr-08-charter-concerns-handling","evidenceIds":[],"rationale":"The requirements forbid changing questions, scope, or difficulty; adding a sixth instructional rung is a packet-level change outside this stage's authority. The correct architect move is to instrument the risk: the design records exactly what observed behavior would confirm the concern and route it upstream."}],"engagementPrediction":"High engagement is predicted through item-05 with the strongest signals at item-04, where tap-to-place construction converts the learner from answerer to builder — the moment most aligned with her demonstrated appetite for harder, more autonomous challenge. Specific predictions: (1) she elects item-06 at better-than-even odds when it is framed as the valley's biggest circuit rather than a test, given the harder-riddles evidence; (2) she uses the hint toggle at least once at item-04 or item-06 and continues afterward, matching her riddle-scaffolding pattern; (3) she audits the item-05 factor-order demonstration closely and accepts it because the light-trace makes the referent swap literally countable; (4) session completion through item-07 approaches ceiling because no failure exit exists and the finale is previewed from second one. The main engagement risk window is item-02, the least autonomous item after the orientation's novelty fades; the greenhouse's skip-counting audio rhythm is the designed mitigation.","falsifyingEvidence":"This design is falsified by any of the following observed outcomes: (1) the learner declines item-06 AND shows disengagement signals (rushed taps, no hint use, no re-counting) beforehand — falsifying the invitation-framing hypothesis built on riddle evidence; (2) repeated incorrect commits at item-04 followed by abandonment rather than hint requests — falsifying the untested retry-resilience hypothesis (asm-06, 0.5) and confirming the charter's flagged need for an intermediate partially-pre-built rung, which this experience cannot add without a sixth instructional item; (3) the learner treats the flicker-and-return feedback as failure (visible frustration, quitting at first miss) — falsifying the neutral-circuit-tester tone choice; (4) confusion at item-05 where the two-order light-trace is read as 'the same equation twice' rather than a referent swap — falsifying the diegetic demonstration of factor order and confirming asm-04's loose-phrasing trust-cost warning; (5) hint-toggle use at item-01 or item-02 where hints are unnecessary — indicating the 'circuit notes' affordance reads as required content rather than optional tool; (6) construct-vs-read accuracy delta at item-04 substantially exceeding the item-03 baseline — the exact charter concern, which would require upstream packet revision, not creative adjustment.","firstAction":"One tap. The learner taps a number choice under 'How many equal groups?' (choices 2, 3, 4, 5). The tap target is at least 88px, works identically for touch and mouse, and the moment it is touched the corresponding count animates in the scene: tapping '4' pulses each of the four trays in sequence with a counting tick, so even the very first action shows the mathematics gripping the world before anything is committed. Both questions (groups, amount per group) are visible simultaneously; nothing is committed until both are answered and the single 'Connect the wire' button is tapped, exactly per the item-01 response contract.","firstThreeSeconds":"Second 0–1: the valley fades in from black, districts as dark silhouettes with faint outlines, the tower pulsing one weak amber light. Second 1–2: the promise line types on ('Wire the circuits. Light the valley.') while the first district — the lunch hall — glows faintly and slides its scene forward: four lunch trays materialize one at a time with a soft placement sound, each visibly holding 3 apple slices. Second 2–3: two large glowing question panels rise beneath the trays — 'How many equal groups?' and 'How many in each group?' — with tappable number choices already lit and inviting. No menu, no tutorial screen, no name entry: the child is looking at a real countable situation and live buttons within three seconds.","firstThreeSecondsNote":"All timings assume asset preload completes before fade-in; a static valley silhouette serves as the loading state so the promise image is present even during load.","interactionContinuity":"One interaction grammar persists across all seven districts: representation on the left, response panel on the right, tap-to-choose with non-committal in-world preview, a single 'Connect the wire' commit, current-flow feedback. What changes is only which parts of the circuit the learner owns — mirroring the packet's monotonic scaffold withdrawal exactly. In items 01–03 the representation is pre-wired and she supplies increasingly many values; in item-04 the same tap gesture that previously selected numbers now places carrots (same input verb, new authority); in item-05 the same toggle gesture judges equations; item-06 chains two commits on one persistent array; item-07 replays her own saved surfaces. Because the grammar never changes, no re-tutorializing is ever needed, and rising difficulty is experienced purely as rising ownership, not new controls.","interactionDemonstration":"The demonstration is diegetic, not a tutorial overlay. When item-01's trays appear, the tower sends a thin animated pulse of light down a wire toward the lunch hall that stops at an open switch — showing wordlessly that answering connects the circuit. As the learner hovers or rests a finger on any number choice, the scene previews that count non-committally (pulsing that many trays or that many slices on one tray), teaching the tap→world mapping through the world itself. The 'Connect the wire' commit button visibly closes the switch when both parts are chosen. By the end of item-01 the learner has experienced the full loop — choose, preview in the world, commit, world responds — with zero instructional text beyond the item's own prompt.","mathAsPower":"The mathematics is literally the power source. Groups × amount-per-group is framed as the valley's electrical law: a circuit only carries current when the number of equal groups and the amount in each group are both named truly. Wrong factors produce a mismatched circuit the world visibly rejects (flicker-and-return), correct factors produce current, and the product is the wattage — the district lights exactly that many lamps, countably. In item-04 the learner's own built array IS the circuit board: her construction physically becomes the wiring diagram that powers the carrot farm. In item-05, judging which equations match is framed as inspecting which of four fuse-cards fit the library's circuit — both valid orderings fit, and the world demonstrates the referent swap by re-tracing the same 30 book-lights first shelf-by-shelf (3 groups of 10) then in the other reading order, total unchanged. The child never does math to earn an unrelated reward; the math IS the mechanism, visibly and causally.","motionDirection":"Motion serves counting and causality, nothing else. Counting pulses are strictly sequential and rhythmic (one object per beat, ~180ms) so skip-counting is physically supported by the animation. Current-flow along wires uses a single bright particle stream, 600–900ms tower-to-district, always completing before feedback text appears so cause precedes explanation. Illumination on success is lamp-by-lamp in product count at an accelerating but still countable cadence. Incorrect flicker is soft and brief (two dim pulses, ~400ms) with zero shake, zero red flash, zero falling/breaking motion. Item-04 placement snaps with a gentle magnetic settle so tap-to-place feels precise without drag skill. No motion ever occurs during the learner's deliberation window except idle ambience (slow tower pulse, drifting clouds) — the world is calm while she thinks. All transitions between districts are a single camera glide ≤1.2s. Every animation is skippable-by-tap after first viewing and respects prefers-reduced-motion by swapping to instant-state changes with the counts still shown discretely.","openingPromise":"A dark valley scene: a signal tower stands unlit at the center of a dim landscape with seven darkened districts (lunch hall, greenhouse, sticker workshop, carrot farm, library, stadium, and the tower itself). A single line of text: 'The valley's power runs on equal groups. Wire the circuits. Light the valley.' The promise is explicit and structural: every correct multiplication visibly restores power to one district, and the learner can see all seven dark districts from second one — the whole scope of the experience is legible at a glance, and the payoff (a fully lit valley) is previewed as silhouette.","payoff":"The finale is consolidation made spectacular, per asm-08. When item-07's meaning statements are committed correctly, the tower's beacon ignites and the camera pulls back to the whole valley at dawn — every district lit, the learner's own 8×6 carrot grid and 3-shelf library visible in miniature glowing exactly as she built and judged them. The tower then replays the full chain as a light-trace across the map, with the learner's own numbers: situation → equal groups and amount in each group → representation → equation → verified product, narrated in one calm line per link ('You found the groups. You built them. You wrote them. You counted them true.'). Nothing generic: every element in the finale is her completed work, per the item-07 validation rule. A final still frame of the lit valley with her two equations (8 × 6 = 48, 3 × 10 = 30) inscribed on the tower serves as the closing image. If item-06 was completed, the stadium's 81 pennants ripple in the finale wind as a visible extra.","progression":["District 1 — Lunch Hall (item-01, opening orientation): name the two governing quantities for 4 trays of 3 slices; maximum support, both quantities as bounded choices, no product demanded, no failure state; establishes the tap→preview→commit loop diegetically.","District 2 — Greenhouse (item-02, Segment 1): representation and equation 5 × 4 = ? both given; learner supplies only the product 20 by counting or skip counting the tulips; stepwise hints become available as an offer.","District 3 — Sticker Workshop (item-03, Segment 2): first array, 6 rows of 7 stickers, no equation shown; learner names both factors with referents before the equation reveals, then supplies 42; exactly one new demand added (array language) while the representation stays given.","District 4 — Carrot Farm (item-04, Segment 3, responsibility-transfer point): words only, no picture; learner builds 8 rows of 6 on the tap-to-place grid, assembles 8 × 6 from factor tiles, supplies 48; system guards structural integrity but never auto-arranges; array or equal-groups builds both accepted.","District 5 — Library (item-05, Segment 4): 3 shelves of 10 books shown; learner judges four fuse-card equations, accepting both 3 × 10 = 30 and 10 × 3 = 30 and rejecting 3 + 10 = 13 and 10 × 10 = 100; the world traces the same 30 lights in both reading orders to show the referent swap without formal property names.","District 6 — Stadium (item-06, Segment 5, strictly elective): offered as 'the valley's biggest circuit — yours if you want it,' curiosity-framed with no competitive language; two-stage 9 × 9 = 81 then 7 × 9 = 63 with a banner covering 2 rows; hints on request only, nothing pushed; exit back to the standard tier at any time with all progress preserved and the stadium showing a reserve-circuit glow rather than darkness.","District 7 — The Tower (item-07, payoff consolidation): the learner's own saved carrot-farm build and library judgment are re-shown unaltered inside the tower; she states what each factor counted in her own 8 × 6 = 48 and in 3 × 10 = 30; unlimited re-answers, no failure exit; correct meaning statements light the tower's beacon and trigger the full-valley finale."],"recovery":"Recovery is designed as re-engagement, not penalty, per the packet's universal reasoning-retry rule. On any miss: (1) the countable representation is re-exposed with one diagnostic element highlighted (one pot's tulips, one array row, one equation card); (2) the learner is asked to re-count or re-decide that one thing — the system never supplies the decision; (3) all committed correct work is preserved, including item-06's stage-one commit if stage two is missed, and item-04's built rows are never erased. Stepwise hints exist behind a small, dignified 'circuit notes' toggle — visually a tool, not a lifeline — surfaced as an available offer in items 02–07 and strictly on-request-only in item-06, matching each item's hintPolicy; no hint tier ever states a withheld product. Because the wrestling-loss evidence establishes only comfort discussing loss (asm-06 at 0.5 confidence), retry tone is deliberately neutral-curious rather than consoling: no 'don't worry!' language, just the circuit tester's calm re-highlight. Item-07 has no failure exit at all: unlimited re-answers, and the valley finale always plays.","replayVariation":"Question content, answers, scope, and difficulty are frozen and never change — replay variation lives entirely in the mutable layer. On replay: district visual skins rotate (night-rain, snow-dusk, autumn palettes), the tower's light-trace finale takes a different path, ambient details vary (different birds, different lamp colors within districts), the item-04 build grid offers a different valid starting camera framing, and the item-06 invitation copy rotates among curiosity framings. The learner's prior session's builds can appear as faint 'blueprint ghosts' in the tower archive, letting her compare her own past constructions — a self-referential novelty that respects her demonstrated sensitivity to repetition without altering any mathematics. If she previously declined item-06, the reserve-circuit glow on the stadium is the one standing invitation that replays fresh.","soundDirection":"Sparse, tactile, and honest. Counting ticks: soft wooden marimba tones ascending slightly per count, so a group of 7 sounds like 7 — audio doubles the countability. Commit: a satisfying switch-clack. Correct current: a warm rising hum resolving to a soft chime as lamps light, one faint pluck per lamp at low volume so the product is audible. Incorrect: a neutral low 'hm' tone and the flicker's soft buzz — deliberately unlike any game-over sound, no descending sad motif, honoring the untested-failure-response caveat. Ambient: quiet night crickets shifting toward dawn birdsong as districts light, giving global progress an audible dimension. Item-06's stadium, if entered, earns a distant crowd-murmur warmth on completion. All narration text is also available as spoken audio on tap (positive small-sample evidence for spoken activities), never auto-played mid-deliberation. Full experience functions with sound off; audio is reinforcement, never information's sole carrier.","stakes":"Diegetic and warm, never punitive: the valley is dark and its districts wait. Each district has a small silhouetted detail that lights only on completion (the greenhouse's tulips bloom in light, the stadium's pennants ripple). The stake is restoration — the learner can always see how much of the valley remains dark and that only she can light it. There is no timer, no lives, no score, no leaderboard, and no comparative framing anywhere, honoring the charter's warning that in mathematics difficulty must read as invitation rather than judgment. The optional item-06 stadium is the valley's biggest, brightest district — the stake of skipping it is only that its lights stay off unless she chooses it, and the valley still counts as fully restored on the standard path (the stadium shows a 'reserve circuit' glow rather than darkness if declined).","title":"The Signal Tower: Power the Valley","upstreamHash":"e5ec527f89190a889bb329d64bd778fefbac5eacb145c81ffcdbc1ff7c82311b","usefulLibraries":["PixiJS (WebGL 2D rendering for the valley scene, current-flow particles, and lamp illumination at 60fps in-iframe)","GSAP (timeline sequencing for counting pulses, current travel, and the finale light-trace)","Howler.js (audio sprites for counting ticks, switch-clack, ambient crossfades, and tap-to-speak narration)","@use-gesture (unified pointer handling so touch and mouse behave identically for tap and tap-to-place)","Zustand (session state: committed answers, preserved builds, district illumination, item-06 election and exit)","canvas-confetti (finale-only celebratory light motes, tuned to the amber palette)"],"visualDirection":"Warm-dark engineering aesthetic: deep indigo night valley, amber and gold circuit light, districts in flat-shaded illustrated silhouette with hand-drawn warmth (no corporate flat-design coldness, no toddler pastels — this must read as competent and slightly grown-up for a nine-year-old who asks for harder problems). Countable objects (slices, tulips, stickers, carrots, books, pennants) are high-contrast, evenly spaced, and never decoratively occluded, so counting is always honest — a hard rule given her logic-auditing. Typography: one rounded geometric sans at generous size; equations in a distinct monospaced 'circuit stencil' face so mathematical objects are visually first-class. Layout locked to 1365×768 with no scrolling ever: representation left ~60%, response panel right ~40%, hint toggle bottom-right, valley progress strip along the top edge at ~48px. Tap targets ≥88px. Color is never the sole carrier of correct/incorrect state (current-flow motion plus icon shape carry it) for accessibility.","worldReaction":"The world reacts causally and countably to every mathematical act. Choice previews pulse the exact count chosen (choosing '7' per row pulses seven stickers in one row). Correct commits: current animates from tower to district, and the district illuminates in exactly the product's count of lamps, verifiable by eye — 12 tray-lights, 20 tulip-lamps, 42 sticker-glints, 48 carrot-row lights in the learner's own layout, 30 book-spines, 81 then 63 pennant-flickers, so the world itself performs the verification each item's correctAnswer demands. Incorrect commits: flicker-and-return at the switch plus the item-specific diagnostic highlight. The valley map accumulates: each lit district stays lit, the tower gains a beacon ring per district, ambient sky lightens stepwise from night toward dawn across the session, so global progress is readable at every moment without any score number. In item-05 the world explicitly demonstrates the factor-order insight by re-tracing identical lights in two orders; in item-07 the tower projects the learner's own prior builds as living circuit blueprints."}},"upstreamHash":"8ce3dc55f20416bc1d6d2c73556f3f817ed8b75da208efbf10da505dfb5b840c","viewport":{"height":768,"width":1365}}
